<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Referenced by package.json build.mac as both `entitlements` and
      `entitlementsInherit`, on top of `hardenedRuntime: true`. Every key here
      switches OFF a hardened-runtime protection, so nothing gets added without
      a reason written down next to it.
    -->

    <!-- Required by Electron/V8: the JIT writes then executes generated code.
         https://github.com/electron/notarize#prerequisites -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      NOT present: com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables.

      It was granted here from the file's first commit (4240be1) with no
      justification, and it is not part of electron-builder's own default
      template. Failure it caused: with that entitlement, DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES
      still works against the signed, hardened app, so anyone with plain
      user-level code execution (a malicious npm postinstall in a project the
      user opens, an agent spawned with --dangerously-skip-permissions) could
      drop an unsigned dylib and have it loaded into the Tars process — which
      then runs with Tars's bundle identity and whatever TCC grants the user has
      approved for it. Nothing in this app reads or sets any DYLD_* variable
      (grep the tree), so the entitlement bought us nothing and cost us the
      protection. Do not re-add it; if a profiler or sanitizer build ever needs
      DYLD_*, use a separate dev-only entitlements file and never ship it.
    -->

    <!--
      Kept deliberately. This is electron-builder's own default for apps that
      ship third-party prebuilt native code:
      https://github.com/electron-userland/electron-builder/issues/3940

      package.json asarUnpacks node_modules/better-sqlite3 (which loads a
      downloaded prebuilds/darwin-*.node, not a locally compiled one) and
      node_modules/node-pty (pty.node plus the separate spawn-helper binary).
      Under library validation the main binary may only load code signed by the
      same Team ID, and this repo has no signing identity configured at all —
      `codesign -dvvv release/mac-arm64/Tars.app` reports
      flags=0x20002(adhoc,linker-signed), TeamIdentifier=not set — so there is
      currently no signed build in which dropping this key could be verified.
      Removing it blind is a launch-time crash in a native require, not a
      hardening win.

      TODO: once a real Developer ID identity and the notarize step are wired
      into the release build, do a `--dir` build with this key removed and
      confirm better-sqlite3 and node-pty still load. If a nested binary fails,
      fix that binary's signature rather than restoring this entitlement.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
